Jacques,

Gerhard Smit here, CTTX in Gqeberha. We engineer owned networks on lodges, reserves and farms; we don't sell packages.

You ran Pumba twice as GM, so you'll know better than anyone where the
reserve's communications fall short: between the lodges, out to the
rangers, and after hours when something happens in the field. On most
reserves that ends up as a patchwork of LTE routers and whatever signal
reaches the lodge, rented month to month.

An owned, monitored backbone across the reserve changes that: every lodge,
gate and camera on one network, with the carrier link coming in at a
single point.

I'd like to run a desk study on Pumba from terrain and public
infrastructure data first: where a backbone can run across the reserve and
how a reliable uplink gets onto it. It tells you what is feasible before
anyone spends money.

If that's useful, the next step is a half-day assessment on site: what you
have, what fails, what it costs you, and what an owned network would look
like. R3,500 ex VAT, credited against the build, and the report is yours
either way. The one-page proposal is attached; the short version is here:
www.cttx.co.za/assessment

